Objective:

  Escalate from a compromised service account to Domain Admin by exploiting Unconstrained Delegation,
  and understand ACL abuse chains as an alternative escalation path.

Unconstrained Delegation — How It Works

  When Unconstrained Delegation is enabled on a service account, the KDC embeds a full copy of the authenticating
  user's TGT inside the TGS it issues for that service. This TGT copy is delivered to the service's host and
  cached in LSASS. If an attacker compromises that host, they can extract cached TGTs (including Domain Admin TGTs
  if a DA authenticated to the service) using Mimikatz and reuse them.
  
  Victim (DA) authenticates to service → KDC embeds DA's TGT in TGS
   → TGT cached in service host LSASS
   → Attacker extracts TGT via Mimikatz
   → Attacker reuses TGT as Domain Admin

  Lab Configuration
   . svc_backup — Unconstrained Delegation enabled
   . IIS running on .15 under svc_backup identity
   . SPN: HTTP/iis.redteamlab.local
   . Goal: trigger a Domain Admin (Administrator) to authenticate to the IIS service, then extract the cached TGT

ACL Abuse Chains (Conceptual Coverage)
Key ACL rights and their abuse paths:

 Right	                      Abuse
___________________________________________________________________________________________________
 GenericAll	                  Full control — reset password, modify any attribute, add to groups
___________________________________________________________________________________________________
 GenericWrite	                Write most attributes — e.g. add SPN to make account Kerberoastable
___________________________________________________________________________________________________
 WriteOwner	                  Take ownership first, then grant yourself GenericAll
___________________________________________________________________________________________________
 WriteDACL	                  Modify the object's DACL — grant yourself any right
___________________________________________________________________________________________________
 ForceChangePassword	        Reset account password without knowing current password
___________________________________________________________________________________________________

Attack direction:
          low-to-high only matters offensively. Find paths where your compromised account has any
          of these rights over a higher-privilege object.

Key Lessons:
  . Unconstrained Delegation is a legacy feature — any service account with it enabled is a high-value target
  . The attack requires triggering a privileged user to authenticate to the service (SpoolSample/PrinterBug in real engagements)
  . ACL abuse chains often span multiple hops in BloodHound — follow the full path, not just the first edge
  . Lab infrastructure issues (IIS/DNS) terminated the hands-on portion; technique marked conceptually complete

 Interview Explanation:

"Unconstrained Delegation causes the KDC to embed a full copy of an authenticating user's TGT inside service tickets
 which gets cached in the service host's LSASS. An attacker who compromises that host can extract cached TGTs including
 Domain Admin tickets and reuse them. I also covered ACL abuse chains where misconfigured AD permissions
 (GenericAll, WriteDACL, WriteOwner) create privilege escalation paths between objects."
